00:34:46.249 --> 00:34:50.020
I think in seventh grade, so like,

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:52.940
he no longer could even
come to public school

00:34:52.940 --> 00:34:55.890
with us and then he was so limited that

00:34:57.270 --> 00:34:59.842
whenever he had the energy to do things

00:34:59.842 --> 00:35:03.070
and we were doing things
with him, it was like,

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:06.420
he had a little bit more
freedom to experience,

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:10.092
I mean he experienced so much of life

00:35:10.092 --> 00:35:13.535
especially given the physical limitations

00:35:13.535 --> 00:35:15.893
from which he suffered.
- Mm-hmm.

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.150
- I don't know, I take
some comfort in that

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:22.070
and I feel honored that we were along

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:25.990
for the ride, literally a
lot of times in his boat.

00:35:25.990 --> 00:35:28.610
But maybe there was some sort of sense of

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:34.270
life is short, go for it.

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:38.810
I think that maybe his
parents had a sense of that.

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:39.643
- Hmm.

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:43.290
- That kinda led to, yeah,
we'll drop you off here

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:45.930
and we trust you to be,
he had a good head head

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:48.163
on his shoulders and he was like,

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:51.540
he was smart about all that type of stuff,

00:35:51.540 --> 00:35:53.850
and we trusted him with
our lives, you know?

00:35:53.850 --> 00:35:55.670
- The only thing I'll
say that makes me think

00:35:55.670 --> 00:36:00.670
that I don't, I mean
maybe they acted that way

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:03.280
without even realizing
it but he was that way

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:06.260
from as far back as I can
remember when I met him

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:10.706
in third grade and he
told stories of Oklahoma

00:36:10.706 --> 00:36:12.383
which is where he moved from.

00:36:14.420 --> 00:36:16.980
All the stuff, in third grade
you're not that old, right?

00:36:16.980 --> 00:36:18.040
You haven't lived a whole lot of life

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:20.240
but he had already done
so many cool things.

00:36:22.527 --> 00:36:26.000
And so I think they would
just, they trusted him

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:31.769
and he was super
responsible and we just did,

00:36:31.769 --> 00:36:33.430
again, it was a different time.

00:36:33.430 --> 00:36:36.670
- Not that we didn't almost
die on the river many times

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.620
but that's once we got our kayaks.

00:36:38.620 --> 00:36:40.360
- But you need that, you need that.

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:41.410
And that's the thing.

00:36:43.445 --> 00:36:47.530
It's so difficult to then try to map that

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:50.060
onto our kids' lives because I don't,

00:36:50.060 --> 00:36:53.870
I think about, you got Lily
driving right now, right,

00:36:53.870 --> 00:36:56.460
she's learning to drive and

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:03.400
when we were 16...

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:07.170
Oh no I guess we were 17 at the time.

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:10.713
Me and you just got into a
car and drove to Indiana.

00:37:11.653 --> 00:37:14.270
(Link chuckles)

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:15.270
From North Carolina.

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:16.830
- Well we were going to a wedding.

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:18.630
- Yeah but I mean--
- I mean we were meeting

00:37:18.630 --> 00:37:19.663
your parents there.

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:22.507
It's just a road right,
but yeah, that's kinda,

00:37:22.507 --> 00:37:25.940
that's a long ways for a 17 year old.

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:29.160
- Especially back then when
it was literally it was a map.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:30.143
We had a map.

00:37:31.917 --> 00:37:33.620
Like a paper map.

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:36.630
- I thought we followed, we
didn't follow your parents?

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:37.635
- No.

00:37:37.635 --> 00:37:38.740
(chuckles)

00:37:38.740 --> 00:37:39.900
- 'Cause that wouldn't be as fun.

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:42.363
I think we talked them
into not following them.

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:45.010
'Cause they drove too.

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:46.600
It's like we'll meet you there.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:50.240
- No 'cause when we got to town

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:53.620
and we met my granddad, remember that?

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:55.740
- Yeah that's right.
- Driving along the road.

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:58.770
Anyway but the opposite
of the question is,

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:00.000
so I don't know what the answer is,

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:01.520
I mean there's lots of
things that I'd like my kids

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:03.250
to be able to experience but I'm also glad

00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:06.610
that they're not doing
anything as risky as we did

00:38:06.610 --> 00:38:09.500
so it's really tough to answer
but what do they get to do

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:11.850
that we kinda wish that we got to do?

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:14.480
- Well the first things
that comes to my mind is

00:38:16.308 --> 00:38:20.060
our kids come to the studio
and they're in videos

00:38:20.060 --> 00:38:25.060
and stuff, I mean I think we
would be eating it up if--

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:26.109
- That's true.

00:38:26.109 --> 00:38:31.109
- If we had access, I mean we're still,

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:34.147
that's why we're so, we're
living our childhood dreams

00:38:34.147 --> 00:38:37.370
that we couldn't even articulate.

00:38:37.370 --> 00:38:40.642
So it's like, man,

00:38:40.642 --> 00:38:45.642
your dad worked at the law school

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:51.050
and they just happened to
have this setup with two VCRs

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:55.590
and you thought well
that was our opportunity

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:57.760
to edit video for the first time

00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.550
but that was like heaven.

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:03.030
It was like I'm going and I got Dad's key

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:05.950
and I'm going into the two VCR room.

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:07.120
- Yeah.
- I mean it's like

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:10.720
think about if it wasn't
two VCRs but it was this.

00:39:10.720 --> 00:39:15.683
Like hey, I can be on my
dad's show or something.

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:19.990
We're filming some stuff,

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:22.630
we film stuff with Shando all the time.

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:26.720
Like gettin' them into it
because they're so stinking cute

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:30.370
and the way that they play
off of each other is hilarious

00:39:30.370 --> 00:39:31.550
because they're so different.

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:33.200
- Well and we did a similar thing

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:36.893
with Locke and Lincoln back,
was it The Mythical Show?

00:39:36.893 --> 00:39:38.200
- Yeah The Mythical Show.
- They came in

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:39.033
and did a segment.

00:39:39.033 --> 00:39:41.610
- Yeah it was like--
- When they were cute.

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:44.150
(Link chuckles)

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:45.010
- But they don't--

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.900
- But they don't wanna
be a part of it now.

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:48.440
- It's not like they're living a dream,

00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:50.523
it's just like they get a kick out of it.

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:53.257
Lily actually said, "You
know what, I'd like to be

00:39:53.257 --> 00:39:54.140
"in a video again."

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:55.000
She still thinks it's kinda--

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:57.350
- Oh yeah 'cause she did
some stuff last year.

00:39:57.350 --> 00:39:58.807
- Yeah.

00:39:58.807 --> 00:40:02.030
She's gonna come in next week
and watch us film some stuff.

00:40:02.030 --> 00:40:03.280
She's excited about it.

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:05.680
I'm glad that they do feel that way

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:08.250
but our minds would have been blown, man,

00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:11.410
if it was like, I don't know,
maybe we wouldn't have cared.

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:13.610
It's like we would have grown up,

00:40:13.610 --> 00:40:17.717
if our dads were like,
worked at the public access

00:40:21.470 --> 00:40:23.643
television studio or something,

00:40:24.893 --> 00:40:26.310
that's kinda what the
equivalent would have been.

00:40:26.310 --> 00:40:29.080
- The first thing that came to my mind was

00:40:29.080 --> 00:40:32.040
the stuff that our kids get to do.

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:36.180
And this is partly because of our jobs

00:40:36.180 --> 00:40:38.841
and partly because of where we live

00:40:38.841 --> 00:40:43.841
but also, this is a much more
travel-oriented generation.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:47.440
It's much more, people fly a lot more,

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:48.663
people go a lot further.

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:51.780
I think about the vacations
that we've been on

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:53.790
with our family, our kids
have been to Australia,

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:58.790
our kids have been to Europe
and Locke's been to Africa.

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:01.760
You're gonna be--
- Lily went to China.

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:03.510
- She went to China--
- With a friend of hers.

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:05.340
- And you're gonna be in
that part of the country,

00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:06.540
part of the world again.

00:41:07.512 --> 00:41:12.512
And our kids get to eat
all this amazing food

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.333
when amazing food for me was Shoney's.

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:18.930
- Yeah you're talking
about amazing food in LA.

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:20.733
- Yeah well and around the world too.

00:41:22.530 --> 00:41:24.560
I don't know, I feel--

00:41:24.560 --> 00:41:26.170
- Yeah it would have
been nice to be spoiled

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:27.737
like our kids have is
basically what you're saying.

00:41:27.737 --> 00:41:29.640
- I'm of two minds about this though

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:34.640
because I kinda think that
the fact that really up until

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:39.173
I graduated college,

00:41:40.488 --> 00:41:42.900
Shoney's was still (clicks tongue),

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:46.410
that was as good as it got for
me, you know what I'm saying?

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:51.410
I think that I'm able to, I
think you adjust pretty quickly

00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:53.576
but I think I'm able
to kind of appreciate,

00:41:53.576 --> 00:41:57.750
we traveled a little bit,
we would go to Georgia

00:41:57.750 --> 00:41:59.480
to see family but--
- Remember that time

00:41:59.480 --> 00:42:02.950
that my mom took me and me to Carowinds?

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:06.090
- Yeah, I'll never forget
it because those kinds

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:09.660
of things were so, they were
anomalies in our experience.

00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:12.310
It was just going to an
amusement park near Charlotte.

00:42:12.310 --> 00:42:13.683
- Pretty awesome.
- Yeah.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:16.521
- Would have blown our minds.
- You wanna go to Carowinds?

00:42:16.521 --> 00:42:17.790
Is that what you're asking?
- Would have blown our minds

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:19.680
to go to Six Flags.

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:22.520
It wouldn't have been that much different.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:25.290
- Yeah so I don't know, in one sense,

00:42:25.290 --> 00:42:27.170
I think what I'm saying is
there's things that come

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:28.900
to mind that I would have liked to do

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:31.640
and I'd like them to
do but at the same time

00:42:31.640 --> 00:42:34.410
I'm kinda glad that I had the life I had

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:36.210
and they have the life they have.

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:37.310
- Yeah, don't have the power to change it

00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:39.650
so let's just reconcile it.
- Yeah just accept it.

00:42:39.650 --> 00:42:43.130
- Jonathan Garlinghouse, if you
guys were to face each other

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:46.163
in a quote, dad sports triathlon,

00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:50.630
and in parentheses he
put darts, pool, bowling.

00:42:50.630 --> 00:42:52.400
Is this a thing or did he make that up?

00:42:52.400 --> 00:42:55.100
Is a dad sports triathlon
a thing and it consists

00:42:55.100 --> 00:42:58.133
of darts, pool, and bowling?

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:02.622
His question is, what
strengths would you bring to it

00:43:02.622 --> 00:43:05.650
and who would win overall?

00:43:05.650 --> 00:43:09.870
Well I mean I am not...

00:43:09.870 --> 00:43:13.000
I'm trying as hard as I can
when I throw darts on the show.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:15.207
- Yep.
- I mean I'm just,

00:43:15.207 --> 00:43:17.030
I just got no chance, man.

00:43:17.030 --> 00:43:21.170
And we're so close to that
board and I'm horrible at it.

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:25.580
- But I don't think your
weakness is the targeting.

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:27.163
- It kinda is.
- I think your weakness is

00:43:27.163 --> 00:43:28.950
the guessing.

00:43:28.950 --> 00:43:31.420
Are you historically that
far from your guesses?

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:36.420
- Yeah, I mean if you were to super impose

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:41.180
like an actual dart board and
the amount of fidelity there,

00:43:41.180 --> 00:43:42.013
I'm telling you right now--

00:43:42.013 --> 00:43:43.040
- Well somebody should do that.

00:43:43.040 --> 00:43:45.140
- I would be horrible at it.

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:48.450
- But we have, well--
- Pool, not great at that.

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:51.069
You're not good at, so you
would beat me at darts.

00:43:51.069 --> 00:43:55.130
- But we've done pool and
bowling on, well okay,

00:43:55.130 --> 00:43:57.660
if we're judging this by performance

00:43:57.660 --> 00:44:01.080
on Good Mythical Morning, I
actually think historically,

00:44:01.080 --> 00:44:03.260
you would win, we played pool one time

00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:07.320
and you were better at it than me.

00:44:07.320 --> 00:44:09.540
I don't think I'm that
bad of a pool player

00:44:09.540 --> 00:44:12.173
but you beat me on that day.

00:44:12.173 --> 00:44:16.280
And then bowling, when we
did the flame bowling--

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:18.803
- I was better.
- At least in the edit,

00:44:18.803 --> 00:44:20.719
you got more of the strikes.

00:44:20.719 --> 00:44:22.580
(Link chuckles)

00:44:22.580 --> 00:44:26.550
- No if we're bowling
one game of bowling...

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:29.760
Is it called a round?

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:31.220
I don't know, a set?

00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:32.820
I don't know what it's called.

00:44:32.820 --> 00:44:34.400
A frame.

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:37.810
I would, I think I'd beat
you, but if we went two,

00:44:37.810 --> 00:44:38.800
my fingers give out.

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:40.980
- But here's the thing though is that

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:45.190
if you isolate this down
to pure, pure competition

00:44:45.190 --> 00:44:48.700
and entertainment value,
it's not being filmed.

00:44:48.700 --> 00:44:51.410
- Mm-hmm.
- And it's just simply,

00:44:51.410 --> 00:44:55.220
I kinda go into a very specific
mode in those scenarios.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:56.960
- Yeah, which sends me into another mode

00:44:56.960 --> 00:44:59.390
so your mode is--
- I get very competitive.

00:44:59.390 --> 00:45:01.860
- I have to win, my--
- I would be tough to beat

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:03.833
if it was just about winning.

00:45:03.833 --> 00:45:06.920
- Yeah and it sends me
into my mode which is

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:09.525
why am I doing this again?
(Rhett laughs)

00:45:09.525 --> 00:45:12.863
I'm searching for a motivation.

00:45:15.281 --> 00:45:18.647
Yeah I'm just like, I really don't,

00:45:19.740 --> 00:45:22.480
even in winning, I'm looking around like

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:25.812
for some sort of an instruction manual.

00:45:25.812 --> 00:45:27.630
- When you win.

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:31.600
- Yeah I just don't really--

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:33.670
- Well the winning is the reward.

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:35.045
- I don't really care that much.

00:45:35.045 --> 00:45:38.550
- It's the winning, that's it.
- I do think that--

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:41.270
- It's not the champagne
that comes afterwards.

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:44.500
- I might could really
work up some gumption

00:45:44.500 --> 00:45:47.800
and I think I could beat you at bowling.

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:50.273
I think that would be a good competition.

00:45:51.850 --> 00:45:53.280
- It'd definitely be close.

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:54.820
- I think like Frisbee golf,

00:45:54.820 --> 00:45:56.400
I think that would be pretty even.

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:59.313
I like to think I'm pretty
good with a Frisbee.

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:02.253
- Yeah, yeah.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:06.730
We did that, we used
to do that in college.

00:46:06.730 --> 00:46:08.913
- But I'd blow you away at napping.

00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:11.510
- Is that a dad, that's a dad thing?

00:46:11.510 --> 00:46:13.973
- I'm just saying, yeah, dad's gonna sit,

00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:18.080
hit the couch and who's
gonna go out first?

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:21.380
I could definitely blow you
out of the water at napping.

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:23.051
- I can't nap competitively.

00:46:23.051 --> 00:46:24.350
That's a given.

00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:27.900
- I mean after we had that
meeting, before we came in here--

00:46:27.900 --> 00:46:29.240
- You snored within 30 seconds.

00:46:29.240 --> 00:46:31.190
- Well yeah I laid down on the couch,

00:46:31.190 --> 00:46:34.239
I was like we got a meeting in 12 minutes.

00:46:34.239 --> 00:46:36.280
I'm gonna take a 12 minute nap

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:40.440
and Jade was sittin' on my
lap and I even had to pee

00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:43.230
and I had to move Jade
because she was on my bladder.

00:46:43.230 --> 00:46:46.280
I had to pee, I had a
dog sleeping on top of me

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:48.108
'cause she's constantly sleeping.

00:46:48.108 --> 00:46:53.108
And I know I fell asleep
because I did that jerk thing.

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:54.700
- You're such a jerk.

00:46:54.700 --> 00:46:56.940
- I did the jerk.

00:46:56.940 --> 00:47:01.490
I actually looked it up, it's
called a hypnagogic jerk.

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:03.140
- Yeah.

00:47:03.140 --> 00:47:04.890
- You said I was snoring, is that--

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:08.670
- No exaggeration within a
minute there was a (snorts),

00:47:08.670 --> 00:47:10.780
there was like a--
- That was when I jerked.

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:12.363
- Oh yeah (chuckles), okay.

00:47:13.770 --> 00:47:18.770
- Also called a hypnic
jerk or sleep starts.

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:20.360
- Sleep starts?
- Sleep starts,

00:47:20.360 --> 00:47:24.110
like I think that's short for startle.

00:47:24.110 --> 00:47:25.460
- Okay.

00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:28.690
Yeah I've had that happen
but in a 12 minute--

00:47:28.690 --> 00:47:29.960
- That's why I'm a little
grumpy right now 'cause--

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:31.776
- A 12 minute--
- A 12 minute nap--

00:47:31.776 --> 00:47:33.370
- It's supposed to help.
- Well it should have been,

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:35.300
I think it should have been
20 minutes is a power nap,

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:36.530
right, not 12 minutes.

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.853
